.TH BUFFER_WRITE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_write \- Write data to a file descriptor

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_unixwrite (int fd, const char* x, unsigned int n);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_unixwrite\fR function is used to write data to a file descriptor. It takes a file descriptor \fBfd\fR, a pointer to the data to be written \fBx\fR, and the number of bytes to write \fBn\fR as parameters.

The function returns the number of bytes written, or -1 if an error occurred.

The \fBbuffer_unixwrite\fR function is intended to be used with the \fBbuffer_read\fR function to implement buffered I/O operations. The \fBbuffer_flush\fR function can be used to ensure that all data is written out before closing the file descriptor.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_unixwrite\fR function to write data to a file descriptor:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int main() {
    fd_type fd = open("example.txt", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0644);
    if(fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    const char* data = "Hello, world!";
    ssize_t n = buffer_unixwrite(fd, data, strlen(data));
    if(n < 0) {
        perror("buffer_unixwrite");
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
.nef